    <page>
        <paragraphs>
            <title>Introduction to the Chain Rule</title>
        <example workspace="2in">
            <introduction>
                <p>
                    Assume that you are on (or analyzing, if that's not your thing) a roller coaster, and you have two functions:
                    <ul>
                        <li>
                            <p>
                                <m>x(t)</m>, which is the position of the car on the track <m>t</m> seconds after the ride begins, and
                            </p>
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            <p>
                                <m>h(x)</m>, the height of the roller coaster at each position <m>x</m> on the track (measured from the starting position)
                            </p>
                        </li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </introduction>
            <task>
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        How would you determine your height <m>t</m> seconds after the start of the ride?
                    </p>
                </statement>
        
            </task>
            <task>
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        What does <m>h'(t)</m> measure?
                    </p>
                </statement>
            </task>
            <task>
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        How else could you compute <m>h'(t)</m>?
                    </p>
                </statement>
            </task>
        
        </example>

        <p>
            The important take-away here is that when we want to combine rates of change, they often multiply. This is particularly relevant for compositions of functions, which we will see with the <em>Chain Rule</em> starting tomorrow.
        </p>
        
            
        </paragraphs>
    </page>
</handout>

<handout component="cur">
    <title>Tuesday 10/6</title>
    <objectives>
        <ul>
            <li>
                <p>
                    Understand the formula for the Chain Rule.
                </p>
            </li>
            <li>
                <p>
                    Use the Chain Rule to differentiate composite functions.
                </p>
            </li>
        </ul>
    </objectives>
    <page>
        <paragraphs>
            <title>Composite Functions</title>

            <p component="b" workspace="3in">
                Before we get into the actual Chain Rule, let's spend a minute reviewing composition of functions, since that's what the chain rule handles. What does it mean to compose two functions?
            </p>
            <p component="a">
                Before we get into the actual Chain Rule, let's spend a minute reviewing composition of functions, since that's what the chain rule handles. What does it mean to compose two functions? Plugging one into the other, output of one is the input of the next. Nesting functions.
            </p>

            <example workspace="2in">
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        If <m>f(x) = x^2 - 5x + 1</m> and <m>g(x) = 7x - 4</m>, what are <m>f\circ g</m> and <m>g \circ f</m>?
                    </p>
                </statement>
            
            </example>
            

        </paragraphs>
    </page>
  
<page>
    <p>
        The important thing for us in this section will be to recognize a function as a composition of simpler functions. Ideally, simpler functions, each of which we directly know how to differentiate.
    </p>

    <example workspace="2in">
        <introduction>
            <p>
                For each of the following functions, write them as a composition of (at least) two smaller functions.
            </p>
        </introduction>
        <task>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    <m>\displaystyle f(x) = (4x^2 + 3x + 7)^9</m>
                </p>
            </statement>

        </task>
        <task>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    <m>\displaystyle g(x) = 2\sin(5x)</m>
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>
        <task>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    <m>\displaystyle h(x) = \sin^2(x^3 - 1)</m>
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>
    
    </example>
    
</page>

<page>
    <paragraphs>
        <title>The Chain Rule</title>

        <p>
            Alright, now back to calculus. How can we differentiate these composite functions? Like we discussed briefly last time, we expect the rates of change to multiply in some sense. What does that actually look like?
        </p>

        <theorem xml:id="thm-chain-rule">
            <title>The Chain Rule</title>            
            <statement>
                <p component="b" workspace="2in">
                   Let <m>g(x)</m> be a function so that <m>g(x) = f(u(x))</m>, or <m>g = f\circ u</m>. Then 
                </p>
                <p component="a">
                    Let <m>g(x)</m> be a function so that <m>g(x) = f(u(x))</m>, or <m>g = f\circ u</m>. Then <md>
                        g'(x) = f'(u(x))u'(x)
                    </md>, or, in Leibniz notation, <md>
                        \frac{dg}{dx} = \frac{df}{du} \frac{du}{dx}
                    </md>.              
                </p>
            </statement>
        </theorem>

        <example workspace="3in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Find the derivative of the function <m>f(x) = (4x^2 + 3x + 7)^9</m>.
                </p>
            </statement>
        <solution component="a">
            <p>
                
            </p>
        </solution>
        </example>
        
    </paragraphs>
</page>

<page>
    <example workspace="2in">
        <statement>
            <p>
                Find the derivative of the function <m>f(x) = 2\sin(5x)</m>.
            </p>
        </statement>
    
    </example>

    <example workspace="3in">
        <statement>
            <p>
                Find the derivative of the function <m>f(x) = 3\cos(x^2 + 2x + 1)</m>.
            </p>
        </statement>
    
    </example>
    
</page>

<page>
    <paragraphs>
        <title>Special Instances of the Chain Rule</title>
        <p component="b" workspace="4in">
            Often, you'll see the chain rule notated in a short-hand way for specific types of functions.
        </p>
        <p component="a">
            Often, you'll see the chain rule notated in a short-hand way for specific types of functions. For example, by the chain rule, we know how to compute <md>
                \frac{d}{dx}\left(f(x)\right)^n
            </md>. Shorthand to <m>u</m>.
            
        </p>
        
    </paragraphs>
</page>

</handout>

