    </page>
    <page>
        <p>
            The chain rule can also be repeated more than once in a given problem.
        </p>
        <example workspace="3in">
            <introduction>
                <p>
                    Compute each of the following derivatives:
                </p>
            </introduction>
            <task>
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        <m>\displaystyle \frac{d}{dx}\left(\sin^2(x^3 - 1)\right)</m>
                    </p>
                </statement>
        
            </task>
            <task>
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        <m>\displaystyle \frac{d}{dx}\left(e^{\cos(3x)}\right)</m>
                    </p>
                </statement>
        
            </task>
        
        
        </example>
        
    </page>
    <page>
        <paragraphs>
            <title>General Exponential Functions</title>
            <p component="b" workspace="2in">
                Using the chain rule, we can compute the derivative of general exponential functions of any base.
            </p>
            <p component="a">
                Using the chain rule, we can compute the derivative of general exponential functions of any base. How can we rewrite <m>b^x</m> as an exponential with base <m>e</m>?
            </p>
        </paragraphs>
    </page>

    <page>
