<handout component="cur">
    <title>Friday 10/9</title>
    <objectives>
        <ul>
            <li>
                <p>
                    Introduce the ideas behind Implicit Differentiation
                </p>
            </li>
            <li>
                <p>
                    Quiz on 3.1-3.3.
                </p>
            </li>
        </ul>
    </objectives>
    <page>
        <paragraphs>
            <title>Implicit Differentiation</title>

            <p>
                Implicit Differentiation is our first main application of the chain rule. It kind of says what it is in the name: finding the derivative of implicit functions. But what does that mean?
            </p>

            <definition xml:id="def-implicit">
                <statement>
                    
                    <p component="b" workspace="2in">
                        An equation is written in <term>explicit</term> form if
                    </p>
                    <p component="a">
                        An equation is written in <term>explicit</term> form if it is written as <m>y = f(x)</m>. If it is not in this form, it can be made to look like <m>f(x,y) = 0</m> or <m>F(x,y) = C</m> for some constant <m>C</m>, and is said to define <m>y</m> <term>implicitly</term> as a function of <m>x</m>.
                    </p>
                </statement>
            </definition>

<p>
    Why might this be useful? Consider the graph of the equation <md> (x^2 + y^2 - 3x)^2 - (1.5)^2(x^2 + y^2) </md> given below
</p>

<image width="60%">
  <prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org"
             label="deriv-implicit-limacon-full-one">
    <xi:include href="../../prefigure/deriv-implicit-limacon-one.xml" />
  </prefigure>
</image>
        </paragraphs>
    </page>

    <page>
        <p>
            <md> (x^2 + y^2 - 3x)^2 - (1.5)^2(x^2 + y^2) </md>
        </p>
        <sidebyside>
            <image width="60%">
  <prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org"
             label="deriv-implicit-limacon-full-two">
    <xi:include href="../../prefigure/deriv-implicit-limacon-one.xml" />
  </prefigure>
</image>
<image width="60%">
  <prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org"
             label="deriv-implicit-limacon-pieces-one">
    <xi:include href="../../prefigure/deriv-implicit-limacon-two.xml" />
  </prefigure>
</image>
        </sidebyside>
    </page>

    <page>
        <p>
            Let's look at an easier example to motivate this process.
        </p>
    <example workspace="4in">
        <statement>
            <p>
                How would you find the equation of the tangent line to the circle <m>x^2 + y^2 = 25</m> at the point <m>(3,4)</m>?
            </p>
            <image width="60%">
  <prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org"
             label="deriv-implicit-circle-one">
    <xi:include href="../../prefigure/deriv-implicit-circle-one.xml" />
  </prefigure>
</image>
        </statement>
        <solution component="a">
            <p>
                Use that it's perpendicular to the radius. Or, solve the equation for <m>y</m> and differentiate.
            </p>
        </solution>
    
    </example>
    
    </page>

    <page>
        <p>
            What if we didn't want to do it that way? Or, what if we couldn't solve out for <m>y</m>? What else could we do?
        </p>
        <p component="b" workspace="4in">
            <nbsp />
        </p>
        <p component="a">
            Just differentiate the equation as a whole. Apply the chain rule on any <m>y</m> terms, then solve for <m>\frac{dy}{dx}</m>. This is the idea of implicit differentiation.
        </p>
    </page>
</handout>


</section>